29:["$","$L38",null,{"type":"multiple","children":[["$","$L39",null,{"title":"If it's one binary, why does the deployment guide use three?","id":"why-three-processes","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Because the roles have different scaling and failure characteristics, not\nbecause the code requires it. A CAS wants durable storage and steady\nmemory; a worker wants CPU and is disposable; a scheduler wants to be\nrestarted freely. Splitting them lets you size and autoscale each one\nseparately. See ",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/operate/production-config","children":"production configuration"}],"."]}]}],["$","$L39",null,{"title":"Can I add a store without touching the scheduler or worker?","id":"add-a-store-in-isolation","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Yes. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge-store"}]," sits below both, and neither knows about concrete\nstore types, with one exception worth knowing: the worker's fast-CAS\npath downcasts to a concrete ",["$","code",null,{"children":"FilesystemStore"}]," and refuses to start if it\nis given anything else. That is covered in\n",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/explanations/worker-execution","children":"worker execution"}],"."]}]}],["$","$L39",null,{"title":"Why does the process panic instead of retrying?","id":"why-panic","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Because the alternative is a process that is running, passing its health\ncheck, and quietly not doing one of its jobs. Every root future is\nsomething the process exists to do; if one ends, the process has nothing\nuseful left to be. Dying is the honest signal, and it is the one your\norchestrator already knows how to act on."}]}]]}]
